// @test: verify-pass
// @level: L1
// @tier: 0
// @tags: proofs, tactics, cases, structured-proof
//
// Pin (T0960) — a `cases` block splits the GOAL, on a scrutinee that may be
// a theorem PARAMETER.
//
// Two separate things were wrong, and together they made writing MORE proof
// turn a provable goal into a failure. Measured on one binary, a ladder over
// the same theorem `em(b: Bool): b || !b`:
//
//     proof by trivial                                   Proved
//     proof { trivial }                                   Proved
//     proof { left; trivial }                             Proved
//     proof { cases b { true => …, false => … } }         FAILED
//
// The goal is a tautology the solver closes in 10ms. Adding a case analysis
// — the most ordinary thing a person writes in a proof — lost it.
//
//   1. The step walker had no goal, so the `Cases` proof-step kind built
//      one out of the SCRUTINEE: `cases b` case-split on the goal `b`
//      instead of on `b || !b`, so the theorem's own goal was never reached.
//
//   2. The scrutinee was looked up among the HYPOTHESES, and `b` is a
//      parameter — "Hypothesis 'b' not found. Available: h0..h0". A
//      parameter is registered in the engine's value tables, not the
//      hypothesis list; and a Bool parameter was not registered at all,
//      because `type_to_sort_and_name` answers `("Bool", None)` for a
//      primitive and the registration site read only the name.
//
// Both halves are needed: fixing either alone measured INERT.
//
// This file is the L1 pin because the defect is not about tactics being
// weak — nothing here needs a clever tactic. It is about a proof structure
// destroying its own goal.

theorem em(b: Bool): b || !b {
    proof {
        cases b {
            true => {
                left;
                trivial
            }
            false => {
                right;
                trivial
            }
        }
    }
}

/// The control that the theorem above is not vacuous: the SAME goal, with
/// no `cases` block, discharged before this fix and must keep discharging.
/// If this one ever goes red the fix has broken the ordinary path, and the
/// one above proves nothing about `cases`.
theorem em_without_cases(b: Bool): b || !b {
    proof by trivial
}

fn main() {
    print("ok");
}
